#{[17592186129893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1061 11204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6d9d3ca6-ae9b-5ac5-a35f-80fa6628ef2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1061 23124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4dac68d-5f9a-5fba-b2c5-3ea5a9ec6475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n  *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*. \n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\n       \n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1065 2500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7f7db883-9d6e-5a67-97d4-20274467a1e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198360 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861