#uuid "2f924b6e-2f47-553e-b2e6-6d98b8c55641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199248 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199249 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199249 #edb/ref 1061 26206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16b2c818-03e6-5801-97b2-0ce8d225c3d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199266 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199266 #edb/ref 1061 42404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199266 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54cb06ef-1ffc-5b15-9147-a1baf70b985c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199266 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199268 #edb/ref 1061 42423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199268 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b07afe2-9993-5d3c-bfe7-b98917224b3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199268 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199314 #edb/ref 1061 44589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "63b8e23b-b4a6-5984-b37f-dab9c1ced3af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199315 #edb/ref 1061 44598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "809a242a-d30e-5e4d-9b3e-fc6725358aac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199450 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n![](images/fe/fefb7b82a6da1421bb96798fe6377e66199b50b8630dc396518927a0f3fe204b.svg)\n\nThe points in the $(u,v)$ plane where $\\mathbf T$ is *locally* non-invertible are known as **critical points**. \n\nWe've already seen that $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible when the Jacobian determinant is zero. Therefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ are the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nLet's compute the critical points of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/8e/8e55b2da9a0c1fcf1ab901d361c50b652c906b92f0fcd4b158793f7782cfe5f4.svg)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4u & -6v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4u\\cdot 1-(-6v)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when the Jacobian equals zero. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u-3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ consist of those points that lie on the line $2u-3v=0$  in the $uv$-plane, shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5c544e3d3ecbdeaf7dc372b40ee0f37cb14e3ba292b8180a263c91103a9efe61.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199450 #edb/ref 1065 4312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f47e688-774b-5c89-a90d-ead52a871598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199450 #edb/ref 1067 "Critical Points of Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199460 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now state the so-called **inverse function theorem** for single-variable functions:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the graph below:\n\n![](images/4d/4dfd8b142b594a2ca4f0e39f9b8ca1e04018bf0efd63f9dc2235bd61e9f9d70e.png)\n\nIn this example, the function $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ and has a nonzero derivative. As a result, $f^{-1}$ exists in a neighborhood of $x=b$ and is continuously differentiable at this point.\n\nOn the other hand, if $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ but $f'(a) = 0,$ then one of two things can happen:\n\n- **Case 1**: The function $f$ does not have a local inverse at $a.$\n\n![](images/04/04ee310e6068e517aec42a67fa83837986f8807e1250b89b52e7a62e3975bb38.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is not one-to-one in a neighborhood of $a,$ so it's not invertible there.\n\n- **Case 2**: The function $f$ has a local inverse at $a,$ but its inverse is not continuously differentiable at $b.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2738a16f999d503fb295f4fd6535a854884468a4d190ffa537a4ec9295490c.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a,$ but $f^{-1}$ is not differentiable at $x=b$ since it has a vertical tangent at this point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199460 #edb/ref 1065 7036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199460 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c45037f7-31c7-5911-a00d-edaa4c5b86e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199460 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199477 #edb/ref 1064 "The inverse function theorem can be generalized to transformations too. We state it below:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf T:U \\to \\Bbb R^2$ be a $C^1$ (continuously differentiable) transformation on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ where *\n![](images/82/829c083606df36014d5b5b078b80c1c0ff1edb72f981880b4a1a6a16affb32dc.svg)\n\n*Further, suppose that $(u_0,v_0)$ is **not** a critical point of $\\mathbf T.$* Then,\n\n- *$\\mathbf T$ is locally invertible at $(u_0,v_0),$*\n\n- *$\\ma